  "drivers": [
    {
      "name": "Post-2018 claim-count trend",
      "direction": "down",
      "effect": -2.3,
      "unit": "millions versus 2023 persistence",
      "evidence": "Comparable current-law counts declined from 20.450 million in 2018 to 17.626 million in 2023; the fitted annual slope is approximately -0.569 million."
    },
    {
      "name": "Permanent $2,500 threshold",
      "direction": "neutral",
      "effect": 0.0,
      "unit": "millions",
      "evidence": "P.L. 119-21 made the §24(h)(6) $2,500 substitution permanent, so the forecast does not include the pre-2025-law reversion to $3,000."
    },
    {
      "name": "Long-horizon demographic and filing uncertainty",
      "direction": "uncertainty",
      "effect": 1.25,
      "unit": "interval-width multiplier",
      "evidence": "The target is four tax years beyond the latest complete observation, while the official series is sample-estimated and sensitive to family composition, earnings, tax liability, and filing behavior."
    }
  ],
  "reasoning": [
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "content": "Tax year 2027 Additional Child Tax Credit total claims under current law"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "content": "Framing: Resolve the first IRS SOI Publication 1304 Table 3.3 estimate for the number of all individual returns claiming the refundable child tax credit or additional child tax credit for tax year 2027. The unit is millions of returns. The forecast is conditional on the $2,500 earned-income threshold remaining operative through December 31, 2027. The registered resolutionDate is December 31, 2029, representing the end of the ledger's official first-release window rather than a specific scheduled publication day."
    },
    {
      "type": "tool",
      "name": "IRS Publication 1304 history fetch",
      "result": "Official Table A counts, in millions of returns: 2017 18.341984; 2018 20.450468; 2019 19.867646; 2020 19.119249; 2021 37.771612. The 2021 observation is a temporary-law outlier because the credit was fully refundable that year."
    },
    {
      "type": "tool",
      "name": "IRS 2022 complete-report fetch",
      "result": "IRS Publication 1304 reports 18.077 million refundable child tax credit or additional child tax credit returns for tax year 2022, restoring the pre-2021 structure."
    },
    {
      "type": "tool",
      "name": "IRS 2023 line-item fetch",
      "result": "IRS Publication 4801 reports 17,626,084 Form 1040 returns with an Additional Child Tax Credit entry on line 28 for tax year 2023. Schedule 8812 applies a $2,500 earned-income threshold and a 15% phase-in."
    },
    {
      "type": "tool",
      "name": "Current-law verification",
      "result": "The current 26 USC §24 text records the 2025 amendments and §24(h)(6)'s substitution of $2,500 for the underlying $3,000 amount. CRS states that P.L. 119-21 made this lower refundability threshold permanent."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "content": "Base rate: Comparable current-law observations 2018, 2019, 2020, 2022, and 2023 are 20.450, 19.868, 19.119, 18.077, and 17.626 million. Mean = 19.028 million, sample standard deviation = 1.184 million, and range = 17.626–20.450 million. Annualized declines are -0.583, -0.748, -0.521, and -0.451 million, averaging -0.576 million per year. The 2021 temporary-law value is excluded."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "content": "Benchmarks: Last-print persistence gives 17.6 million. A linear current-law trend gives 15.3 million for 2027. Sparse expanding-window checks produce approximate MAE of 0.594 million for persistence and 0.217 million for the trend, so the trend is selected while its interval is deliberately widened."
    },
    {
      "type": "math",
      "content": "OLS on (year, millions) = {(2018,20.450468),(2019,19.867646),(2020,19.119249),(2022,18.077),(2023,17.626084)} gives slope -0.569 million/year and 2027 point 15.27, rounded to 15.3. Historical comparable-level sigma = 1.184. Four-year/policy/sampling widening factor = 1.25. 80% half-width = 1.28 × 1.184 × 1.25 = 1.89; bounds = 15.3 ± 1.9 = 13.4–17.2 million. The corresponding 90% half-width is 1.645 × 1.184 × 1.25 = 2.43, giving 12.8–17.7 million."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "content": "Prior/update/interval: Prior = 17.6 million last-print persistence using the 2023 IRS observation. Selected model = current-law linear trend over five comparable 2018–2023 prints, which moves the point -2.3 million from persistence because the declining pattern is repeated and outperforms persistence in sparse walk-forward checks. Policy update = 0.0 million because current law already permanently retains the $2,500 threshold. Interval method = empirical comparable-level dispersion widened 1.25 for the four-year horizon, statutory-condition risk, sample estimation, and filing behavior; implied 80% bounds are 13.4–17.2 million."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "content": "Counter-consideration: The trend sample is short, skips 2021 because of temporary law, and could overstate decline if the 2022–2023 reduction reflects transitory filing behavior. A stable qualifying-child population, stronger low-income employment, or more taxpayers exhausting the nonrefundable credit could keep claims near the 17.6 million persistence benchmark."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "content": "Tail stress: Above 17.2 million would require a material reversal, such as unexpectedly strong growth in eligible low- and middle-income families, a shift from nonrefundable CTC to ACTC caused by lower tax liabilities, or administrative changes increasing claims without changing the statutory threshold. Below 13.4 million would require a substantially faster decline in qualifying-child returns, employment or earnings patterns moving families outside the refundable formula, stricter identification enforcement, or a large change in filing behavior."
    },
    {
      "type": "final",
      "content": "Forecast: 15.3 million returns, with an 80% interval of 13.4 to 17.2 million."
    }
  ]
}
